  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + 0.5 z^2) / 2, drawn at 0.4 times the loss, with contour lines. Steps along x shrink by a factor of 1 - 2.5 × rate each time, so learning rates above 0.8 make the x swings grow.",
    "Each step is -rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian, centred on zero and added to both slope parts, with spread 1.5 × sqrt((64 - b) / (63 b)) for batch b out of 64 points. It is exactly 0 at 64. With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows are steps drawn at 3 times their real length, the same scale for both. The purple arrow is the next mini-batch step, the way the ball will move. The dark arrow is the step the full 64-point data would give.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed sequence, so Reset replays it. New run, keep last path uses the next noise values and keeps the finished path as a ghost. Running takes 5 steps a second at 1× speed.",
    "A run finishes after 40 steps. It is diverged when the loss goes past 5 (its value at the steep rim) or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. Then the ball and path are drawn at the rim, and the Loss readout keeps the true value."
  ]
}